3. PYDANTIC SCHEMAS (`schemas.py`) — STRICT INPUT & OUTPUT VALIDATION
================================================================================
Pydantic uses `BaseModel` and `Field` to enforce business rules at the API door. Best practice is separating **Create** schemas (what the user sends) from **Response** schemas (what the API sends back).

```python
from pydantic import BaseModel, Field
from typing import List, Optional, Literal
from datetime import datetime

# 1. Menu Item Schemas
class MenuItemCreate(BaseModel):
    name: str = Field(..., min_length=2, max_length=100, example="Paneer Tikka")
    category: Literal["Main Course", "Sides", "Drinks", "Deals"]
    kitchen_station: Literal["Tandoor", "Curry", "Drinks"]
    price_half: Optional[float] = Field(None, gt=0)
    price_full: float = Field(..., gt=0, example=260.00)
    is_veg: bool = True
    allergens: Optional[str] = Field(None, example="Dairy")
    deal_badge: Optional[str] = Field(None, example="Deal of the Day")
    prep_time_mins: int = Field(15, ge=1, le=120)

class MenuItemResponse(MenuItemCreate):
    id: int
    is_available: bool

    class Config:
        from_attributes = True  # Allows reading directly from SQLAlchemy ORM objects

# 2. Customer Order Item & Order Creation Schemas
class OrderItemCreate(BaseModel):
    menu_item_id: int = Field(..., gt=0)
    quantity: int = Field(..., ge=1, le=20)
    portion_size: Literal["Half", "Full"] = "Full"
    spice_choice: Literal["Mild", "Medium", "Hot"] = "Medium"

class OrderCreate(BaseModel):
    table_id: int = Field(..., gt=0)
    items: List[OrderItemCreate] = Field(..., min_length=1)

# 3. Call Waiter & Review Schemas
class CallWaiterCreate(BaseModel):
    table_id: int = Field(..., gt=0)
    request_type: Literal["Water", "Napkins", "Bill", "Other Help"]

class ReviewCreate(BaseModel):
    table_id: int = Field(..., gt=0)
    bill_id: int = Field(..., gt=0)
    rating: float = Field(..., ge=1.0, le=5.0)
    comment: Optional[str] = Field(None, max_length=500)
```

